{"name":"Venetian Lace","hex":"#f7edda","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"venetian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of Venetian blind or alternative letter-case form of Venetian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venetian"},{"term":"lace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light fabric containing patterns of holes, usually built up from a single thread.","A cord or ribbon passed through eyelets in a shoe or garment, pulled tight and tied to fasten the shoe or garment firmly."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fasten (something) with laces.","To interweave items."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Point de Venise","blocks":[{"text":"Point de Venise is a Venetian needle lace from the 17th century characterized by scrolling floral patterns with additional floral motifs worked in relief. By the mid-seventeenth century, it had overtaken Flemish lace as the most desirable type of lace in contemporary European fashion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Venise_kant_CBY-11.jpg/330px-Venise_kant_CBY-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_de_Venise","wikidata":"Q2022619"},{"title":"Lace","blocks":[{"text":"Lace is a decorative openwork textile made without the use of pre-existing fabric. Lace can be made by hand or machine. Various techniques can be employed to form lace, each having a distinct category. Lace can also be used to refer to other types of openwork fabric such as cutwork or filet, which use pre-existing fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG/330px-Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lace","wikidata":"Q231250"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common